{
  "_comment": "End-to-end timelines of the whole bridge (src/app.js and everything it wires together) against a fake panel and a real MQTT broker (Mosquitto 2, shared by all scenarios: each scenario runs under a topic prefix of its own, which the harness adds to everything the bridge publishes and subscribes to and which every topic below leaves out; the broker accepts any client and holds no retained message under the prefix at the start). Each row of a group is one scenario: the bridge is started, then 'steps' are played in order. Setup: 'login' holds the bridge env USERNAME/PASSWORD, the session value the panel hands out and the u/p the panel accepts for that session (an independent copy of a crypto.json vector, so this file stays self-contained); panel has requireLogin on and the default six zones Predsien, Lava izba, Prava izba, Spalna, Obyvacka, Chodba, status tables of 32 zeros, useraccess [1, 0]; time is simulated: 'advance_ms' moves the clock of the bridge's own timers (poll 1000 ms, keep-alive 3000 ms, MQTT connect timeout 5000 ms) and then waits for the real network effects. Row keys: name, known_bug?, note?, panel?, broker?, start?, steps?. panel: zones ([enabled, name] pairs), statuszone, useraccess, accept_login (false: every login is rejected), down (true: panel is not reachable at startup), faults (list of {path, kind, times?} active from the start; times omitted = for ever). Fault kinds: http_500 (HTTP 500), reset (connection reset before any response), hang (request never answered), login_page (HTTP 200 with the login page), garbage (HTTP 200 with a page that has no status tables), wrong_title (HTTP 200 with a well-formed page titled something else than 'Paradox IP Module'), page (HTTP 200 with the fault's own 'page': an inline string or {file: path relative to tests/}). broker: retained ({topic: payload} the broker already holds, published retained by another client before the bridge starts, replayed on SUBSCRIBE). start: ready (default true: wait until the broker has acknowledged both command subscriptions; false: startup is expected to fail or hang) and expect (checked once startup is over). Step actions, applied in this order within a step: panel_status ({statuszone?, useraccess?, alarms?} the panel serves from now on), panel_fault (same shape as a fault), panel_expire_session, panel_down, mqtt_command ('arm' | 'disarm' | {topic, payload}: 'arm' is topic paradox/command/arm with an empty payload; published, not retained, by another client to the broker, which delivers it to the bridge's subscription), advance_ms. Expectations (expect) describe what happened DURING that step, i.e. since the previous check; absent keys mean 'nothing': mqtt_published (messages the bridge published, in order, as another client subscribed to the scenario's topics sees them; the command topics, where only that client publishes, are left out), panel_requests (request lines the panel received, in order; at startup absent means not checked), panel_requests_any_order (the same compared as a multiset, for timers firing in the same tick), panel_request_counts ({request line: how many times}, for long stretches of time), process_exit (exit codes passed to process.exit, in order), logged_error (true: at least one error-level log entry was written during the step, false: none; log text is otherwise not contractual, but a failure described as 'only logged' must be logged), and, as an absolute state at the end of the step, broker_retained ({topic: payload, or null for none}: what a client subscribing now gets from the broker's retained store). Besides, the bridge publishes every message retained: at the end of every scenario each topic it published holds the last payload as the broker's retained message. A bug pinned on purpose carries known_bug (catalog id in tests/README.md); those scenarios are in known_bugs/system_scenarios.json, in the same groups. All message payloads are hand-written, nothing is computed by the code under test.",
